<reference_puzzle index="8" source="eval" puzzle_id="35ab12c3" lines="73-88">
<description>
Complete outlined shapes and carry attached shadow pixels along their edges.

Input structure:
- bg = background. Each active color marks vertices of an 8-direction aligned outline.
- A start vertex can have adjacent non-bg, different-colored shadow pixels.
- Other colored pixels may be distractors unless they lie on valid drawable edges.

Rule:
- For each color, order its pixels by polar angle around their centroid to form a cycle.
- Draw only horizontal, vertical, or 45-degree edges whose interiors are clear.
- From the chosen start vertex, copy adjacent shadow offsets along each drawable edge
  when the parallel path is in bounds and clear.

Key insight: The main outline is reconstructed first, then the start vertex's neighbor offsets define parallel shadow strokes.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="train" puzzle_id="2de01db2" lines="28-41">
<description>
Invert each row's native foreground color against the black background.

Input structure
- A black-background grid whose rows each have one native foreground color plus optional distractor colors.
- The native row color is the most frequent non-bg color in that row; if tied, its run starts leftmost among tied colors.

Rule
- For each row independently, turn pixels of the native row color into bg.
- Turn every other pixel in that row, including bg and distractor colors, into the native row color.

Key insight
- Distractor colors are not preserved objects; they mark cells that participate in the binary complement of the row's native color.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="eval" puzzle_id="271d71e2" lines="9-22">
<description>
Move bordered containers toward same-colored destination markers, charging gray cells to orange.

Input structure:
- bg = 6 (magenta). Black-bordered (0) rectangles with gray (5) / orange (7) interiors.
- A head sprite (9) sits adjacent to one container edge; a matching destination sprite lies along the movement axis.

Rule:
- Each container translates toward its destination along the head-to-dest axis.
- Steps = min(head-to-dest distance, number of gray interior cells).
- Gray cells become orange from the head-facing side inward, one per step.

Key insight: Gray cell count caps movement — a container stops short if it runs out of gray cells to charge.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="train" puzzle_id="5d588b4d" lines="10-28">
<description>
Expand the top-left horizontal bar into a wrapped sequence of increasing and decreasing foreground runs.

Input structure:
- The grid has a background color and exactly one foreground color.
- All foreground pixels form one contiguous horizontal bar in the top row.
- The bar starts at the left edge; its length is the control value n.

Rule:
- Build a one-dimensional sequence of foreground runs with lengths
  1, 2, ..., n, n-1, ..., 2, 1.
- Separate adjacent runs by one background pixel.
- Wrap the sequence row-major across the input width and pad the final row
  with background pixels.

Key insight:
The output preserves the input width; the bar length controls the run-length
diamond, and the apparent 2D pattern is just that 1D sequence wrapped into rows.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="12" source="train" puzzle_id="66ac4c3b" lines="57-72">
<description>
Complete the sparse-color reflected copy of the foreground motif across its matching marker row or column.

Input structure
- The grid has one background color and two foreground colors.
- One foreground color appears only as a sparse marker row or column.
- The other foreground color contains the same sparse marker positions on a parallel row or column, plus the motif on the far side of the sparse markers.

Rule
- Keep the original grid unchanged.
- Reflect every motif pixel of the larger foreground color across the two parallel marker lines.
- Paint the reflected motif pixels with the sparse marker color.

Key insight
- The two equal marker rows or columns are paired landmarks: the sparse-color marker line borders the source motif, and the matching source-color marker line anchors the reflected copy.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

